<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Republic of the Congo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Congo-Brazzaville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Republic of the Congo is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> as its capital. As the French colony of Middle Congo it was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, and it became independent on 15 August 1960. It was a Marxist-Leninist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> from 1969 to 1991 and depends heavily on oil exports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo.jpg" alt="Republic of the Congo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Republic of the Congo lies on the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>, west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, along the Equator. It is bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and the Angolan exclave of Cabinda, and has 170 kilometres of beaches on the Atlantic. The southwest is a coastal plain drained by the Kouilou-Niari River, and the interior is a central plateau between two basins. The climate is consistent year-round, with a dry season from June to August.</p><p>The country is named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, whose name derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, a Bantu kingdom at its mouth around the time the Portuguese first arrived in 1483 or 1484. It is a distinct country from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>, is named for the colony's founder, Pierre Savorgnan de Brazzà. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> it was known as the French Congo or Middle Congo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples mostly displaced and absorbed the earlier inhabitants, the Pygmy peoples, about 1500 BCE. By the 13th century there were three main federations of states in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo Basin</a>: the Seven Kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> dia Nlaza in the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpemba">Mpemba</a> to its south, and a confederation of Vungu, Kakongo and Ngoyo to the west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. The Tio Kingdom formed in the 14th century. The association of kingship with smithing is believed to have originated among the Tio before spreading to Loango and Kongo.</p><p>The Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a> reached the mouth of the Congo in 1484, and trade grew between the inland kingdoms and European merchants, who dealt in commodities, manufactured goods and enslaved people. The area north of the river came under French sovereignty in 1880 through Pierre de Brazza's treaty with the Tio king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makoko-iloo-i">Iloo I</a>, and Iloo's widow, Queen Ngalifourou, upheld its terms. In 1908 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> as federal capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-ocean-railway">Congo–Ocean Railway</a>, built after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, has been estimated to have cost at least 14,000 lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Republic of the Congo became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1960 and was a Marxist–Leninist state under the name People's Republic of the Congo from 1969 into the early 1990s. Its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>. French is the official language, with Kituba and Lingala as national languages, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> are the largest ethnic group, roughly half of the population. Most of the population lives in the southwest, in Brazzaville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a> and the towns along the railway that joins them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Republic of the Congo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Loango was created by Kongo people on the Atlantic coast north of the more powerful Kingdom of Kongo, in what is now the western <b>Republic of the Congo</b>, southern Gabon and Cabinda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingship survives as a ceremonial office in the <b>Republic of the Congo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tio">Kingdom of Tio</a></li><li><blockquote>Middle Congo became the autonomous <b>Republic of the Congo</b> in November 1958 under the priest-politician Fulbert Youlou, and gained full independence on 15 August 1960, keeping Brazzaville as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a></li></ul></section>
